      "text": "To find out how many times Terrell needs to lift the 15-pound weights to lift the same total weight as lifting the 20-pound weights 12 times, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the total weight lifted with the 20-pound weights.\n2. Determine how many times Terrell needs to lift the 15-pound weights to match this total weight.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the total weight lifted with the 20-pound weights.**\n\nFirst, find the weight lifted in one set of lifts with the 20-pound weights:\n\\[ 20 \\text{ pounds/weight} \\times 2 \\text{ weights} = 40 \\text{ pounds} \\]\n\nSince Terrell lifts the weights 12 times:\n\\[ 40 \\text{ pounds/set} \\times 12 \\text{ sets} = 480 \\text{ pounds} \\]\n\n**Step 2: Determine how many times Terrell needs to lift the 15-pound weights to match 480 pounds.**\n\nLet \\( x \\) be the number of times Terrell needs to lift the 15-pound weights. The total weight lifted with the 15-pound weights will be:\n\\[ 15 \\text{ pounds/weight} \\times 2 \\text{ weights} \\times x \\text{ sets} = 30x \\text{ pounds} \\]\n\nWe need this total weight to be equal to 480 pounds:\n\\[ 30x = 480 \\]\n\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{480}{30} = 16 \\]\n\nTherefore, Terrell must lift the 15-pound weights \\(\\boxed{16}\\) times to lift the same total weight.",
