      "text": "To determine how many times Terrell must lift the two 15-pound weights to lift the same total weight as lifting the two 20-pound weights 12 times, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the total weight of the weights lifted in one complete set of lifting pairs.\n2. Determine the number of complete lifting pairs Terrell can lift with two 15-pound weights.\n3. Calculate how many times Terrell must lift the two 15-pound weights to match the total weight lifted in 12 lifting pairs of the 20-pound weights.\n\nFirst, find the total weight lifted in one complete set of lifting pairs:\n\\[ 20 \\text{ pounds/weight} \\times 2 \\text{ weights} = 40 \\text{ pounds} \\]\n\nNext, determine how many complete lifting pairs Terrell can lift with two 15-pound weights:\n\\[ \\frac{40 \\text{ pounds}}{15 \\text{ pounds/weight}} = \\frac{40}{15} = \\frac{8}{3} \\approx 2.67 \\]\n\nSince Terrell can only lift whole numbers of lifting pairs, we take the integer part of \\( \\frac{8}{3} \\), which is 2. This means Terrell can lift 2 complete lifting pairs with two 15-pound weights, which gives:\n\\[ 2 \\times 40 \\text{ pounds} = 80 \\text{ pounds} \\]\n\nNow, to find out how many times Terrell must lift the two 15-pound weights to lift 80 pounds, we divide the total weight lifted by the weight of one lifting pair of 15-pound weights:\n\\[ \\frac{80 \\text{ pounds}}{15 \\text{ pounds/weight}} = \\frac{80}{15} = \\frac{16}{3} \\approx 5.33 \\]\n\nSince Terrell can only lift whole numbers of lifting pairs, we take the integer part of \\( \\frac{16}{3} \\), which is 5. However, we know from the division that Terrell can actually lift 2.67 lifting pairs, which means he needs to lift the weights 3 times to get close to 80 pounds. To be exact, he needs to lift the weights 5 times to exert enough force to match the total weight lifted by the lifting pairs of 20-pound weights.\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{3} \\]",
