      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine how many times Terrell must lift two 15-pound weights to lift the same total weight as he would with two 20-pound weights, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total weight lifted with two 20-pound weights:**\n    - Each weight is 20 pounds.\n    - Terrell lifts the weights 12 times.\n    \\[\n    \\text{Total weight lifted with 20-pound weights} = 2 \\times 20 \\times 12\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    = 40 \\times 12\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    = 480 \\text{ pounds}\n    \\]\n\n2. **Set up the equation for the total weight lifted with two 15-pound weights:**\n    - Each weight is now 15 pounds.\n    - Let \\( x \\) be the number of times Terrell lifts the two 15-pound weights.\n    \\[\n    \\text{Total weight lifted with 15-pound weights} = 2 \\times 15 \\times x\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    = 30x \\text{ pounds}\n    \\]\n\n3. **Equate the total weights lifted in both scenarios:**\n    \\[\n    30x = 480\n    \\]\n\n4. **Solve for \\( x \\):**\n    \\[\n    x = \\frac{480}{30}\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    x = 16\n    \\]\n\nTherefore, Terrell must lift the two 15-pound weights \\(\\boxed{16}\\) times to lift the same total weight he would have with two 20-pound weights.",
